# The two MCP tools asked about the *dialect*, which is the gap this session
# exists to close (ADR-0349).
#
# `mcp.jsonl` beside this file pins the protocol thoroughly -- the lifecycle,
# the descriptors, both error kinds -- and names `pasjson.pas`,
# `symbols_module.pas` and `hello.pas`, none of which carries anything the
# language gained after its text model. So an agent's `outline` was never asked
# about a trait, a task, a channel, an owned pointer, an optional or a slice.
#
# It cost a crash reaching a user. ADR-0338's traits put two node kinds into a
# block's declaration list, `DumpSymBlock` read them through the procedure arm,
# and `--dump-symbols` stopped the compiler on any source with a `trait` in it
# -- so `outline` answered with the one line printed before it died, which
# reads exactly like a file that declares one thing. 888 cases were green.
#
# What this pins is therefore not the framing, which the session beside it
# already does: it is that the two tools **answer** about every construct this
# language has. A feature added later belongs in workspace/dialect.pas, and
# this golden is what says whether the server still knows about it.
{"jsonrpc":"2.0","id":1,"method":"initialize","params":{"protocolVersion":"2025-06-18","capabilities":{},"clientInfo":{"name":"probe","version":"1"}}}
{"jsonrpc":"2.0","method":"notifications/initialized"}

# The outline of a source declaring the dialect's surface. A trait and an
# implementation are declarations of the block and are deliberately *not*
# lines in an outline: a trait's routine names have no defining-point in the
# block containing it (AP 6.7.9.2), and an implementation's are reached only
# through the trait -- so what an editor draws is the type, and the routines
# under it are not names a reader can jump to here.
{"jsonrpc":"2.0","id":2,"method":"tools/call","params":{"name":"outline","arguments":{"path":"%ROOT%/lsp/sessions/workspace/dialect.pas"}}}

# And what the compiler says about it, which is nothing: the fixture compiles
# and runs, so a diagnostics answer that is not empty means the dialect moved
# under the server rather than that the fixture is wrong.
{"jsonrpc":"2.0","id":3,"method":"tools/call","params":{"name":"diagnostics","arguments":{"path":"%ROOT%/lsp/sessions/workspace/dialect.pas"}}}
